<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 4 – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes on Our Plates</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Square Cracker.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Label common items using a model to copy from. Interactive: Write simple phrases with errors on a drawing or postcard.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can label food items in drawings with simple words (e.g., writing "round" under a picture of an orange). Can write simple phrases with guidance, describing food shapes (e.g., "This is a square cracker."). Can copy simple sentences about food shapes with teacher support (e.g., copying the word "juice box" under a picture of a juice box).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and initiates a quick "Shape Hunt" using realia and flashcards of food. The teacher holds up a round orange and asks, "What is this? What shape is it?" Students respond chorally "Orange! Round!" The teacher repeats with a square cracker, banana, and a lemon, reviewing previously learned vocabulary and shapes. The teacher also asks, "Do you like to eat a banana?" (Yes/No).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a square cracker and says clearly, "This is a square cracker." The teacher writes "square cracker" on the board, pointing to each word as they say it. The teacher explicitly models the /s/ sound in "square" and the /k/ sound in "cracker" and /r/ sound in "round", encouraging students to repeat chorally. The teacher then draws a round apple and writes "round apple" next to it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple questions:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this cracker round?" (Students shake heads or say "No, square!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What shape is the orange?" (Students point to round objects or say "Round!")</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher reinforces the answers and provides additional examples as needed, asking students to point to items matching a given shape or word.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content:</b> The teacher presents a large "Food Shapes Chart" showing various food items (orange, cracker, banana, lemon, juice box) with their corresponding shapes and simple labels written clearly beneath them (e.g., "round orange," "square cracker," "oval banana," "yellow lemon," "juice box"). The focus is on the written words alongside the images.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A colorful "Food Shapes Chart" or large flashcards with images of food items (orange, cracker, banana, lemon, juice box, pineapple, mango) and simple, clear written labels (e.g., "round orange", "square cracker", "oval banana", "juice box").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying food shapes: Identifying and describing foods by shape (e.g., "The cracker is square."). Describing food: Using simple words to describe the colors and shapes of fruits and other foods, such as "orange" and "cracker".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a labeled food item on the chart and asks, "What does it say?" (Students try to read the word).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks students to point to the word "round" on the chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Is this a square lemon?" (No.) "What shape is the lemon?" (Oval/Round).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing simple adjective + noun phrases (e.g., "round apple," "square cracker," "juice box") and labeling items with single words. Vocabulary: pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, juice box, round, square, oval, big, small. Grammar: Adjective + noun.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Tracing and Copying:</b> Students receive a worksheet with dotted outlines of shape words (e.g., "round," "square") and food names (e.g., "cracker," "orange," "banana"). First, they trace the words. Below each traced word, there is an empty line for them to copy the word independently. For example: "r o u n d" (dotted) -> ______ (empty line).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Labeling with a Word Bank:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of various food items (e.g., an orange, a cracker, a juice box, a banana, a lemon). Below each picture, there is a blank line. At the top of the worksheet, a word bank is provided with words like: "round," "square," "oval," "orange," "cracker," "banana," "lemon," "juice box." Students use the word bank to select and write the correct label (food name or shape description) under each picture. For instance, they write "round" or "orange" under the orange picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Pair Interaction - Simple Phrase Writing and Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a simple drawing of a plate with two food items (e.g., a square cracker and a round orange). On the board, the teacher writes model sentences like "This is a ______ ______." and provides a word bank of shapes and food items. With teacher guidance and the provided models, each student in the pair attempts to write one simple phrase or sentence to describe one of the food items on their drawing (e.g., "This is a square cracker." or "This is a round orange."). After writing, partners check each other's work for legibility and correct copying, providing gentle peer support.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual writing and drawing activity: "My Healthy Snack Plate."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students receive a blank sheet of paper or a pre-printed plate outline. They will draw two to three healthy food items (e.g., a round orange, a square cracker, an oval banana, a juice box) on their "snack plate." Below each drawn item, students will write a simple label describing its shape or name, copying from the word bank and sentence models displayed on the board (e.g., "round orange," "square cracker," or "This is a juice box.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> "It's a Square Cracker." and Scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates." Students apply their knowledge by creating their own visual representation of food shapes and labeling them, reinforcing the idea of identifying and describing food in everyday contexts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> The primary focus is on students' ability to *write* (copy and label) the correct simple words and phrases to describe the shapes and names of food items they have drawn. Emphasis is on legibility and correct word selection from models, promoting early writing fluency.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist to monitor students' ability to copy and label during the "My Healthy Snack Plate" activity. The teacher circulates, providing individual feedback and support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Healthy Snack Plate" drawing with their labels. The teacher collects these for review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Legibility:</b> Labels are clearly written and easy to read.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Accuracy of labeling:</b> Correct food names (e.g., "cracker," "orange," "banana," "juice box") are used.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of shape words:</b> Correct shape words (e.g., "square," "round," "oval") are used for descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effort:</b> Student demonstrates engagement in drawing and writing.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw a happy face, a neutral face, or a sad face on their paper to indicate how well they feel they understood and completed the labeling task. Additionally, they complete a simple sentence orally or by pointing: "I can write the word _______." (e.g., "cracker," "round").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new food words did you write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult for you when writing about the food shapes?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The teacher considers: "What worked well in helping students write simple labels and phrases?" (e.g., the tracing activities, clear models, individual support). "What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students struggling with letter formation or word recognition?" (e.g., more direct letter practice, breaking down words into sounds, smaller group instruction). The teacher notes which vocabulary items were easiest/hardest for students to write.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate their understanding of food shapes and names by creating a class "Healthy Snack Poster" or a "My Favorite Food" booklet. They will use the writing skills practiced today to label and describe different foods, integrating all four skills (listening, reading, speaking, writing) into a final communicative project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>